it was an announcement most people in  Sri Lanka had been waiting for IMF staff  and the authorities have reached staff  level agreement to support Sri Lanka's  economic policies with a 48-month  Arrangement under the so-called extended  fund facility  of about 2.9 billion US Dollars Sri  Lanka asked for IMF help in March after  its economy phase collapse and it ran  out of money for food medicine cooking  gas and fuel  tens of thousands of Sri Lankans were  forced to queue for Essentials  the crisis led to months of protests  that forced former prime minister  Mahindra rajapaksa to resign and his  brother president Kota bear to flee the  country  news of an agreement on a bailout has  brought a sense of relief to many here  in Sri Lanka but some observers say it  may be premature to celebrate We Stand  ready to  put this agreement to our management and  then to our if management approves it to  then put it forward to the executive  board as soon as these conditions that I  mentioned are satisfied so we we do need  to await now for these conditions to  take place the main condition is for Sri  Lanka to come up with a workable plan to  pay back the money it owes  the IMF is also asked to increase taxes  an end to subsidize electricity and Fuel  and stringent efforts to Stamp Out  corruption the government has already  begun implementing some of those reforms  through an interim budget  tax management must play an important  role in increasing Revenue including  increasing tax collection tax compliance  and preventing tax evasion I propose  making it mandatory for everyone over 18  years to register with the Inland  Revenue Department irrespective of their  annual income and tax Hall Days  2.9 billion dollars from the IMF will  make a huge difference to Sri Lanka but  in order to secure those funds it must  come up with a proper plan of how it  will pay its mountainous debts for the  people of Sri Lanka that can't come fast  enough Fernandez Al Jazeera Colombo
 

